            "message": "{{#is_alert}}\n## P2 · Pods Cannot Be Scheduled\n**WHAT:** Pods Cannot Be Scheduled: availability signal detected by threshold analysis.\n**WHERE:** {{value}} · env `prod` · region `global`\n**SERVICE:** `kubernetes-platform` (kubernetes / kube_pod, tier0)\n**BUSINESS IMPACT:** The service still functions but measurably worse; a subset of traffic or users is failing.\n**WHY IT TRIGGERED:** Sustained Pending is a scheduler verdict (insufficient resources, taints, quota) — a state, not a level.\n\n**OWNER:** cloud-engineering · support 24x7 · this does not page\n**RUNBOOK:** attached to this monitor as `Pods Cannot Be Scheduled` — open it from the monitor's Runbook asset.\n**AUTOMATED DIAGNOSTICS:** workflow `diag-k8s-workload` has run; its output is attached to this event.\n**RELATED EVENTS:** correlated on `kubernetes-nodes.prod.kubernetes-platform` — deployments, infrastructure changes and platform events from the last 30 minutes are attached as context.\n**SLO / ERROR BUDGET:** `slo-k8s-workload-availability` · this condition consumes error budget\n{{/is_alert}}\n{{#is_warning}}\n## Pods Cannot Be Scheduled — warning\nApproaching the alert condition on {{value}}. Investigate before it becomes P2.\n{{/is_warning}}\n{{#is_no_data}}\n## Pods Cannot Be Scheduled — NO DATA\nTelemetry stopped arriving for {{value}}. Treat this as a telemetry-health incident: an unmonitored resource is worse than an alerting one.\n{{/is_no_data}}\n{{#is_recovery}}\n## Recovered · Pods Cannot Be Scheduled\n{{value}} returned to normal. Error-budget impact recorded against `slo-k8s-workload-availability`. No action required; the correlation group closes when every child recovers.\n{{/is_recovery}}\n{{#is_warning_recovery}}\nWarning cleared for {{value}}.\n{{/is_warning_recovery}}",
